!id: zlAnpCeH3o
!front: What is the algebraic definition of the **Cross Product**?
!back:
Consider two vectors \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \) in the 3-dimensional space, \( \mathbf{a} = \langle a_1, a_2, a_3 \rangle \), \( \mathbf{b} = \langle b_1, b_2, b_3 \rangle \).

The **Cross Product** \( \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} \) is the determinant

\[ \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \begin{vmatrix}
    \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
    a_1 & a_2 & a_3 \\
    b_1 & b_2 & b_3
\end{vmatrix} \]

which expands to

\[
    \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} =
    (a_2 b_3 - a_3 b_2) \mathbf{i} -
    (a_1 b_3 - a_3 b_1) \mathbf{j} +
    (a_1 b_2 - a_2 b_1) \mathbf{k}
\]
~~~

!id: ab8XVzqdg3
!front: What is the geometric definition of the **Cross Product**?
!back:
Consider two vectors \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \) in the 3-dimensional space, \( \mathbf{a} = \langle a_1, a_2, a_3 \rangle \), \( \mathbf{b} = \langle b_1, b_2, b_3 \rangle \).

The **Cross Product** \( \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} \) is a vector that:

- is perpendicular to both \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \) (so orthogonal to the plane containing them),
- has magnitude equal to the area of the parallelogram spanned by \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \):
\[ \|\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b}\| = \| \mathbf{a} \| \| \mathbf{b} \| \sin \theta \]
where \( \theta \) is the angle between \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \), and
- has direction given by the right-hand rule.

![The Cross Product a x b, perpendicular to the plane spanned by a and b, whose magnitude equals the area of the parallelogram they span](cross_product.svg)
~~~

!id: 4wCxwXidGy
!front: Why is \( \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{b} = \mathbf{0} \), for nonzero \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \), equivalent to \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \) being parallel?
!back: From the geometric definition, \( \|\mathbf{a}\times\mathbf{b}\| = \|\mathbf{a}\|\|\mathbf{b}\|\sin\theta \). For nonzero \( \mathbf{a}, \mathbf{b} \), this vanishes exactly when \( \sin\theta = 0 \), i.e. \( \theta = 0 \) or \( \theta = \pi \) — the vectors point in the same or exactly opposite direction, which is precisely the condition for \( \mathbf{a} \) and \( \mathbf{b} \) to be parallel (one a scalar multiple of the other).
~~~

!id: V2TTEVlBd6
!front: Does the cross product satisfy the associative law \( (\mathbf{a}\times\mathbf{b})\times\mathbf{c} = \mathbf{a}\times(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c}) \)? If not, construct a counterexample.
!back: No. Counterexample using standard basis vectors: \( (\mathbf{i}\times\mathbf{i})\times\mathbf{j} = \mathbf{0}\times\mathbf{j} = \mathbf{0} \), but \( \mathbf{i}\times(\mathbf{i}\times\mathbf{j}) = \mathbf{i}\times\mathbf{k} = -\mathbf{j} \). Since \( \mathbf{0} \ne -\mathbf{j} \), the cross product is not associative.
~~~
